{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794997651,"nonce":"8uoM+cuLaY7cKi5f+Bdw+50ZGxY=","title":"PARDISO returns a less accurate solution without a report when a pivot was perturbed and the system is not singular","message":"When MKL PARDISO perturbs a pivot and the system is not singular, `_pardiso_check_factorization` lets the solve through (its residual is below `_SINGULAR_REL`) and nothing is reported. The returned vector can then be several digits less accurate than a pivoting LU: on a 3-D Taylor-Hood Stokes system with 12 perturbed pivots the field agreed with `backend=\"host\"` to 2e-7 only.\n\nThe cuDSS path handles the same situation in `_cudss_check_factorization`: iterative refinement to `_REFINE_TARGET`, and a report when the result lands between `_CLEAN_REL` and `_SINGULAR_REL`. The PARDISO path has neither.\n\nScaling and weighted matching for the symmetric indefinite types removed the perturbed pivots on saddle-point systems, so the known case is gone. The gap remains for any other operator on which PARDISO perturbs a pivot (a general matrix, a nearly singular one).\n\nWanted: the PARDISO check refines or reports like the cuDSS one. A test can drive it with a factorization whose pivot was replaced, as `test_guard_recovers_a_perturbed_pivot_by_iterative_refinement` does for cuDSS.","files":null}]}